#import "template.typ": *

#let metadata = (
  title: "Introduction to Navigation",
  unit: "Cross Country Endorsement | Lesson 1",
  author: "C.Moroney",
  version: "v1.0.0",
)

#let lesson = (
title: [#metadata.title]

,aim: [
To introduce the basic methods of navigation — pilotage and dead reckoning — and the in-flight workflow needed to navigate.
]


,overview: [
This is the first lesson of the Cross Country Endorsement. The student is introduced to the two primary navigation methods (pilotage and DR) and to the disciplined work cycle (CLEAROFF) that underpins all subsequent lessons. The student will plot a short, simple route on a chart; the instructor will use an EFB to populate heading and ETI only (other fields are introduced progressively). The focus is workflow, chart-reading technique, operating the nav log, and pilotage to stay clear of CTA/SUA. The route should demonstrate *both* methods: reasonably long straight legs for DR, and legs requiring pilotage to remain clear of controlled or special-use airspace. The EFB is introduced from the outset as a *supplement* to — not a replacement for — DR and pilotage.
]

,learning_outcomes: [
#text(size:12pt)[*Knowledge*]
- Name the two primary visual navigation methods (pilotage and dead reckoning) and describe when each is appropriate.
- Recall the in-flight work cycle and the checks completed after each waypoint (CLEAROFF).
- Identify key features of a VNC/VTC: controlled airspace and aerodromes; Special-use airspace; topographic and geographic features; chart legend symbols.
- Explain the documentation and planning requirements for a Day-VFR flight away from the vicinity of an aerodrome.
- Recall that the EFB/GPS is a supplement to DR and pilotage, not a substitute.
#text(size:12pt)[*Skills*]
- Build and plot a simple route on a chart and record turning points on the nav log.
- Fly the aircraft on a constant heading and constant altitude.
- Intercept the departure track within 5 NM of the departure aerodrome and record the departure time.
- Update the nav log progressively as the flight proceeds.
- Position-fix using the chart-to-ground technique.
#text(size:12pt)[*Non-Technical Skills*]
- [TEM] Interpret NOTAM and weather information to make a go/no-go decision .
- [SA] Maintain effective lookout and airspace awareness, especially near CTA/SUA steps.
- [HF] Organise the cockpit so chart, nav log and EFB are accessible and secured.
- [TEM] Prioritise tasks using "aviate, navigate, communicate".
]

,theory_knowledge: [
- Reading a map: true direction from a map *(Pg 132)*; measuring distance and scale *(Pg 138)*
- Map reading; reading from the ground to the map *(Pg 189–190)*
- Classification of airspace *(Pg 260)*; avoiding controlled airspace *(Pg 275)*
- Documents to be carried *(Pg 245)*
- Types of charts *(Pg 239)*
]

,ground_brief_topics: [
Total time: 75 mins \
Equipment required: VNC/VTC for the route, ERSA, nav log, plotter/ruler, EFB, current NOTAM and relevant weather forecasts \

*Non-Technical Skills* (5 minutes)
- \[TEM\] Weather and NOTAM interpretation for a go/no-go decision.
- \[SA\] Airspace and traffic awareness; chart orientation in flight.
- \[HF\] Cockpit organisation and management of chart, log and EFB.
- \[TEM\] Task prioritisation — aviate, navigate, communicate.

*Charts and documentation* (15 minutes)
- Types and purpose of VNC, VTC, WAC, ERC-L, PCA; chart legend and symbols.
- Where to find airspace steps, SUA, and aerodrome data.

*Navigation methods* (10 minutes)
- Pilotage vs dead reckoning; when to use each.
- Read from chart-to-ground; Time → big picture → medium picture → small picture.

*Plotting and the nav log* (30 minutes)
- Selecting a suitable route; plotting; recording turning points.
- Selecting and highlighting ground features to serve as visual markers for CTA and SUA.
- How the EFB will populate heading and ETI for this lesson; intent is to do this manually in later lessons.

*The work cycle* (10 minutes)
- On turning point passage:
  - Note Time
  - Conduct a gross error check
  - Conduct a CLEAROFF check
  - Plot position on chart
  - Make any corrections required (1 in 60 or timing)
  - Fly the aircraft!
  - At ETA -5, start looking for next turning point
  - Over turning point, start the cycle again

*EFB as a supplement* (5 minutes)
- The EFB supports, but does not replace, DR and pilotage (v8 §1.8 introduction).
]

,ground_tasks:[
#text(size:12pt)[*Instructor*]
- Review weather, NOTAM and fuel separately, confirming the flight is viable.
- Conduct the ground briefing and introduce the nav log.
- Guide the student through route selection, introduce effective route selection principles
- Use an EFB to provide wind-corrected headings and ETI; populate only those columns, explaining that all of this will be calculated manually by the end of the course.
- Demonstrate plotting one leg and chart-reading technique.
#text(size:12pt)[*Student*]
- Complete IMSAFE self-assessment and confirm fitness to fly.
- Review weather and NOTAM and brief the instructor on the go/no-go decision.
- Prepare a list of turning points and record them on the nav log.
- Plot the route on the chart with instructor guidance.
]

,in_flight_tasks:[
#text(size:12pt)[*Demonstration*]
- Departing on the first leg: noting and writing down the time of departure.
- Calculating ETA for the first waypoint during a CLEAROFF at top of climb.
- Following the DR heading and comparing ETA with ATA at the waypoint.
- Pilotage: identifying *and verifying* a landmark, reading chart-to-ground.
- Position fixing using Time → big picture → medium picture → small picture.
#text(size:12pt)[*Student Practice*]
- Fly a constant heading (±10°) and constant altitude (±200 ft).
- Intercept the departure track within 5 NM of departure.
- Complete a CLEAROFF after each turning point with prompting.
- Identify and verify at least two landmarks per leg.
- Update the nav log (ATA, heading flown) progressively.
- Keep chart reading to a minimum — fly the aircraft first. (Instructor manages radios and fuel log this lesson.)
]

,common_errors:[
- *Head-down / over-reliance on the chart*: Fly the aircraft first; reference the chart at pre-selected points. DO NOT TRACK CRAWL.
- *Chart-to-ground*: Always read from chart to ground, not ground to chart.
- *Wandering heading*: Pick a distant feature on the nose and hold it; trim hands-off.
- *Missing the departure time*: Make "time and heading" the first action after departure.
- *Late or skipped CLEAROFF*: Build the work cycle as a habit at every turning point and top of climb.
- *Expectation bias when identifying features*: Verify a landmark with a second, independent feature before committing.
]

,post_flight:[
- *Student Self-Assessment*: Student describes what went well and the challenges (eg. "I was worried about losing position, so I constantly wanted to reference the chart").
- *Instructor Feedback* (5–10 min):
  - Evaluate against the standards below (S / C / P / NC).
  - Discuss TEM/SA application (eg. early recognition of an airspace step).
- *Scenario Reflection*: How does the work cycle change as legs get shorter or airspace gets busier?
- *Next Steps*: Assign homework. See below.
]

,standards_for_progression:[
- Describes the difference between DR and pilotage and when to use each.
- Interprets major topographic features and airspace on the chart and, with assistance, position-fixes with reasonable accuracy.
- Maintains heading ±10° and altitude ±200 ft for sustained periods.
- Recalls and begins to apply the CLEAROFF work cycle (with prompting).
- *Competency stage*: target *S — Solo standard* development. Record as S (meets the standard to progress toward solo navigation training), C (competent to pre-flight-test standard), or NC (Not Competent — further training/practice in the nominated sub-element). NC in a sub-element does not by itself prevent progression to the next lesson.
]

,safety:[
- *Weather*: Visible horizon; ceiling and visibility comfortably above VFR minima; light winds preferred for the first navex so heading-holding is achievable.
- *Altitude*: Cruise at a suitable VFR level clear of CTA steps; minimum 1,500 ft AGL en route.
- *Emergency Procedures*: Maintain a continuous awareness of the nearest suitable landing area and forced-landing options along track.
- *TEM*:
  - Monitor for student overload; pause navigation tasks and revert to "aviate" if stress/fatigue appears.
  - Maintain airspace separation from CTA/PRD with a generous buffer.
  - Monitor traffic, particularly near aerodromes and common VFR routes.
- *Suggested Routes*: YHEC-YDUN-CPMN-YCAB (TGL)-DBO-UNI-YHEC | YHEC-Rathdowney-YMUR (TGL)-SBK-YHEC
- *Aircraft Limits*: Operate within POH/AFM
]

,homework:[
- Read Bob Tait's RAAus Cross Country Endorsement:
  - Classification of airspace (Pg 260); avoiding controlled airspace (Pg 275); documents to be carried (Pg 245).
  - Reading a map: true direction from a map (Pg 132); measuring distance and scale (Pg 138).
- Prepare for next lesson (*Maintain Track*): read magnetic direction and variation (Pg 134–135) and flight planned track, heading and drift (Pg 164–165).
- Prepare a basic route: plot the route for next flight, avoiding controlled airspace and SUA. Measure tracks and distances and record on a navigation log. This will be used as the basis of the next briefing.
- Reflect: "On today's route, which legs were best suited to DR and which to pilotage, and why?"
]
)

#show: body => lesson_plan(metadata, lesson, body)
